{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n`workflow/steps.py::GATE_POLICIES` (`default`/`strict`/`advisory`) fails a workflow step by\nexact string membership of `finding[\"severity\"]` in a fixed list. Research confirmed this\n`gate` step is NOT wired into any of the three real production gate workflows (plan-review,\ncode-review, completion-verification each use their own bespoke `_decide`/`_reconcile` ops\ninstead) -- `steps.gate` is exercised only by a retained sample workflow\n(`workflow/examples/review_skeleton.yaml`) and a schema-round-trip test fixture\n(`tests/e2e/fixtures/roundtrip-demo.yaml`), both of which feed it plain, severity-only\nfindings (a generic finder+verify pass, not `pass3_decide` output) with no `priority`/\n`decision` field at all. Migrating `fail_on_severity` to a priority-only check would silently\nmake the demo/fixture gate never fail (every finding would default to `priority: 0.0`), losing\nits illustrative value with no live behavior actually fixed (nothing real depends on this\ngate's severity check today).\n\n## Approach\nADD priority-threshold support to `GATE_POLICIES` ALONGSIDE the existing `fail_on_severity`\n(expand, not a replacement) so the gate step works correctly for both today's severity-only\ninputs (the demo/fixture) and any future caller that supplies `pass3_decide`-shaped findings\ncarrying `priority`: each policy gains a `priority_threshold: float | None` key\n(`None` = never fails on priority, matching `advisory`'s empty `fail_on_severity: []`).\nConcrete values: `default: 0.95` (`review_kernel.decide.DEFAULT_BLOCK_THRESHOLD` -- the same\nsource of truth `pass3_decide` uses for its own BLOCK decision, imported not duplicated as a\nliteral); `strict: 0.5` (a concrete, lower-than-default value -- `strict` is meant to catch\nmore than `default`); `advisory: None` (never fails on priority, matching its existing empty\n`fail_on_severity: []`). The `gate` step (`steps.py:215-239`) fails a finding if EITHER its\n`severity` is in the policy's `fail_on_severity` list (unchanged behavior) OR its `priority`\n(when present) is `>= priority_threshold`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each policy in `GATE_POLICIES` gains a `priority_threshold` key: `default: 0.95` (import\n  of `review_kernel.decide.DEFAULT_BLOCK_THRESHOLD`, not a duplicated literal), `strict: 0.5`,\n  `advisory: None`. `fail_on_severity` is NOT removed from any policy.\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/workflow/test_steps.py` passes unchanged for every existing\n  severity-only test case (`test_gate_fails_on_high_severity`,\n  `test_gate_strict_fails_on_any_finding`, `test_gate_advisory_never_fails`,\n  `test_gate_unknown_policy_falls_back_to_default`) -- proving this is additive, not a\n  behavior change for findings that only carry `severity`.\n- [ ] A new test proves the additive priority path using the `default` policy at two priority\n  values (NOT `strict`, whose `max_findings: 0` already fails any non-empty finding set\n  regardless of priority and so cannot isolate the priority-threshold effect): a finding with\n  no `severity` key and `priority: 0.99` fails `default`; the same shape with `priority: 0.5`\n  does not.\n- [ ] A new test confirms `strict` still fails on a priority-only finding purely via its\n  existing `max_findings: 0` clause (a consistency check that the additive `priority_threshold`\n  field does not interfere with `strict`'s pre-existing behavior).\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/workflow/test_review_skeleton.py` passes unchanged (the demo workflow\n  that actually exercises `steps.gate` end-to-end keeps working exactly as before, since its\n  severity-only findings never trigger the new priority path).\n\n## Testing\nTier: in-process unit test against the real `gate` workflow step function. Oracle: the\nstep's returned pass/fail verdict for a finding carrying `severity` only, `priority` only, or\nneither, under each named policy. Contrast case (test-design.md sec.6, config-effect): run a\nseverity-less, priority-only finding through `default` at `priority=0.99` (fails, `>=0.95`)\nand at `priority=0.5` (passes, `<0.95`) -- the two outcomes differing proves the `priority_threshold`\nread path is real, not merely parsed. Regression check: the existing severity-only test suite\nand the `review_skeleton.yaml` end-to-end test both stay green, proving additive-not-breaking."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788143088226715407,"uuid":"bd20830f-6748-43dc-a4f0-8c6389e639f9"}